# Case study — visual capture

When Geo works on **The Vaults of Corhaven** (`board-game/v3`) and the turn produces something **meaningful for a portfolio** (new/changed screens, flows, components, HUD states, or design-system updates), finish by documenting it visually—not only in code or prose.

## Meaningful (capture)

- New or redesigned screens, modals, or HUD layouts
- Online lobby steps, in-match states (your turn vs waiting), toasts, errors
- Style-guide / interface-inventory updates
- Notable before/after UX (reference git commit in caption)

## Skip capture

- Typo-only, refactors with no UI change, dependency bumps, pure docs unrelated to UI

## Capture steps

1. **Run the UI:** `python3 -m http.server 8765` from repo root, or production URL for online/WebSocket flows.
2. **Browser:** Use `cursor-ide-browser` — navigate, set mobile viewport via CDP when relevant, snapshot to interact, `browser_take_screenshot` with filename under `docs/case-study/screenshots/`.
3. **Sync:** Run `./scripts/sync-case-study-screenshots.sh` (screenshots land in Cursor temp first).
4. **Log:** Append a session table row to `docs/case-study/visual-archive.md` (file link, screen name, 1-line design note, commit SHA if applicable).
5. **Timeline:** If the change is a product milestone, add a one-line link to the visual in `docs/case-study-development-log.md` under **Visual archive** or the relevant timeline phase.

## Naming

`YYYY-MM-DD-##-kebab-description-{desktop|mobile}.png` — increment `##` per session.

## Online / XR

- Static server cannot host WS: capture join/title locally; use **deployed** app for create room, kingdom banners, waiting room.
- Quest passthrough: capture when hardware or simulator is available; note limitation in archive.

Keep capture count modest (1–3 images per request) unless Geo asks for a full audit.
